# Every variable this service reads, with no values.
#
# Configuration is environment-only. There is no configuration file, and nothing
# in the code reads one — so a password cannot end up in a committed file by
# being put where the topology lives. Copy this to a gitignored .env for local
# work; in deployment these are the process environment and nothing else.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connections
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The aliases this service configures, comma-separated. An alias listed here whose
# variables are missing or unusable is a startup error: an alias that silently
# vanished would look, to the agent, exactly like one it was never told about.
#
# An alias name may hold letters, digits, hyphens and underscores, must not begin
# with a digit, and becomes part of a variable name in upper case with hyphens
# turned into underscores — so the alias "sales-warehouse" is configured through
# CERBERUS_DB_SALES_WAREHOUSE_*. Every other character is refused, and the dot in
# particular: a dot is what CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_DATABASES below puts between an
# alias and one of its databases, and refusing it in a name written here is the
# whole reason a derived name can never collide with a declared one.
#
# The names an agent actually calls are not always the names written here. An
# alias that lists databases is replaced by one connection per database, each
# named <alias>.<database> — see CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_DATABASES below.
CERBERUS_DB_ALIASES=

# For each alias, replace <ALIAS> with its upper-case underscored form. All five
# are required.
#
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_ENGINE     one of: mysql, postgresql, sqlserver
#                                exact and case-sensitive; there is no default
#                                dialect, because guessing one would mean
#                                tokenizing a statement by rules the server does
#                                not use
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_HOST
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_PORT       1-65535
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_USER
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_PASSWORD   never logged, never in an error, never in a
#                                result; an empty value counts as unset, because
#                                a blank password is far likelier to be a broken
#                                secret injection than a deliberate choice
#
# Required on PostgreSQL, optional on MySQL and SQL Server:
#
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_DATABASES  the databases this alias exposes, comma-
#                                separated as CERBERUS_DB_ALIASES is. Each name
#                                is trimmed; an empty element or the same name
#                                twice is refused at startup rather than skipped,
#                                because "a,,b" is a typo far more often than it
#                                is a way of writing two databases.
#
#                                Each listed database becomes its own connection,
#                                named <alias>.<database>. So
#                                CERBERUS_DB_CRM_DATABASES=sales,billing gives
#                                the agent crm.sales and crm.billing, and no
#                                alias crm at all. A one-element list derives
#                                too — CERBERUS_DB_CRM_DATABASES=sales is
#                                crm.sales — because keeping the parent's name
#                                for one database would mean that adding a second
#                                one silently renames the first alias, and an
#                                agent learns about a renamed alias by being told
#                                the alias it just used is unknown.
#
#                                Unset is a different configuration, and the
#                                engines disagree about it. MySQL and SQL Server
#                                accept it: the alias stays one connection under
#                                the name declared above with no database chosen,
#                                which on MySQL means no default schema and on
#                                SQL Server means the login's own default
#                                database, and on both means the login reads
#                                whatever it has permission for through a
#                                qualified name. PostgreSQL refuses to start,
#                                because a connection there is bound to one
#                                database by the protocol and has no
#                                cross-database query, so a connection with no
#                                database has nothing to read — and the driver
#                                sets no default of its own, which would leave
#                                the server defaulting the database to the user
#                                name.
#
#                                What the list is NOT, on MySQL and SQL Server,
#                                is a boundary. It decides which connections
#                                exist and what the list_databases tool reports,
#                                and it does not stop a read of a database that
#                                is not on it: the gate refuses USE, but nothing
#                                restricts a qualified table reference, so
#                                SELECT * FROM otherdb.tbl on MySQL and
#                                SELECT * FROM otherdb.dbo.tbl on SQL Server are
#                                approved and return rows whenever the login below
#                                has permission on otherdb — with or without a
#                                list, and whatever is on it. Grant that login only
#                                what it should read; on those two engines its
#                                permissions are the boundary, not this
#                                variable. On PostgreSQL the list IS a boundary:
#                                a database not on it has no connection and no
#                                alias, and cross-database queries do not exist.
#
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_DATABASE   RETIRED, and refused rather than ignored. It was
#                                replaced by the plural above; rename it and keep
#                                the value, remembering that the alias the agent
#                                names becomes <alias>.<database>. The refusal
#                                exists because ignoring the old spelling would
#                                give a MySQL alias a working connection to no
#                                database and no hint that the name in it was
#                                never read, and would tell a PostgreSQL operator
#                                that a variable is missing while they are looking
#                                at one they had set.
#
# Optional:
#
# CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_TLS        one of: disable, require, require-insecure
#                                Unset leaves the driver's default in force.
#                                "require" verifies the server's certificate;
#                                "require-insecure" encrypts without verifying,
#                                which is what an on-premise server with a
#                                self-signed certificate needs. Treat "disable"
#                                as putting the credential on the wire in the
#                                clear, and do not choose it on a link where the
#                                login must not be observable. Whether SQL
#                                Server's driver encrypts the login packet even
#                                under "disable" is unresolved in both
#                                directions — the driver's source says it does
#                                and a live measurement here is not consistent
#                                with that. See the comment on TLSDisable in
#                                internal/db/mssql.go for what was measured. The
#                                advice above is the reading that is safe under
#                                either answer.

CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_ENGINE=
CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_HOST=
CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_PORT=
CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_DATABASES=
CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_USER=
CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_PASSWORD=
CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_TLS=

# The derived names follow the alias as written in CERBERUS_DB_ALIASES, not the
# upper-case form in these variable names: with CERBERUS_DB_ALIASES=example and
# CERBERUS_DB_EXAMPLE_DATABASES=sales,billing, the aliases the agent calls are
# example.sales and example.billing, and nothing is configured under the name
# example alone. list_connections is what tells the agent those names.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which databases exist. Nothing to set here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The list_databases tool takes an alias and reports the databases that alias's
# login can see, with the engine's own system databases dropped:
# information_schema, mysql, performance_schema and sys on MySQL; postgres,
# template0 and template1 on PostgreSQL; master, model, msdb and tempdb on SQL
# Server. It runs one fixed statement per engine on the alias's existing
# connection, through the same gate and the same bounds as a query, and it opens
# no connection and caches nothing. What it returns are database names and not
# aliases: only the names in CERBERUS_DB_ALIASES, with their derived suffixes, are
# names a query can be run against.
#
# It exists for the case CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_DATABASES cannot be filled in from
# memory: an operator who cannot see a host's databases cannot enumerate them, and
# an agent working against a MySQL or SQL Server alias with no databases listed
# needs to know what to qualify its table names with.
#
# It reports what exists rather than what is reachable. On MySQL, SHOW DATABASES
# silently omits the schemas the login has no privilege on, so an account with no
# grants and a server with no databases give the same empty answer. On SQL Server
# a database whose name is visible but whose access was revoked still appears. On
# PostgreSQL pg_database is readable by everyone, so the answer can name databases
# this login could not open — and, separately, a database that is not in that
# alias's CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_DATABASES has no connection and no alias, so the
# agent cannot query it until somebody adds it here and restarts.
#
# Startup touches no database, so a login that cannot run its engine's discovery
# statement is not a startup failure: it shows up on the first list_databases
# call, as the ordinary agent-facing error, naming no credential and no host.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bounds. These apply to every alias, and every one of them has a default.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The most rows one result may carry. Enforced by stopping the iteration, never
# by editing the statement — a modified statement is a statement the gate did not
# approve. Default 1000.
CERBERUS_DB_ROW_CAP=

# What the *server* is asked to enforce: PostgreSQL's statement_timeout and
# MySQL's max_execution_time are both set from it. SQL Server has no
# statement-level server bound, so there the context deadline is the bound — and
# on that engine a context deadline does tear the session down. Default 20s.
CERBERUS_DB_QUERY_TIMEOUT=

# How much longer than CERBERUS_DB_QUERY_TIMEOUT the context deadline is, on the
# engines that have a server-side bound. It exists so the server's own bound
# expires first: on MySQL a context deadline demonstrably does not stop the query
# — the driver closes the socket and never sends KILL QUERY — so which mechanism
# fires must not be a race. Default 5s.
CERBERUS_DB_TIMEOUT_GRACE=

# How long a statement may wait for a lock. It matters most on SQL Server, where
# our reads are the only thing that could block a third party's own users and
# where the default is to wait forever. Default 3s.
CERBERUS_DB_LOCK_TIMEOUT=

# Bounds establishing a connection, which over a VPN is a separate and likelier
# failure than a slow query. Default 10s.
CERBERUS_DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=

# Per-alias pool ceiling. Small on purpose: the pool exists to avoid a handshake
# per call, not to achieve concurrency against someone else's production server.
# Default 4.
CERBERUS_DB_MAX_CONNS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The MCP server. Every one of these has a default too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the HTTP listener binds, as host:port. Default 127.0.0.1:8080.
#
# The default is loopback because this service is meant to be published by
# something in front of it. The Google authentication below is now the boundary
# that decides who may read every alias above — this variable is not the only
# thing standing there any more — but it is still the outer half of it, and the
# half that costs nothing to keep: a request that never reaches the listener is
# one nothing has to authenticate. An address reachable from the internet also
# hands anybody a way to spend this process's validation budget, because a
# rejected token is never cached and every attempt at one is a fresh request to
# Google against a small number of concurrent slots — so a burst of nonsense
# tokens is refusals for the operator's own agent. Binding anywhere else is
# therefore a deliberate act and reads like one in a diff: the process logs a
# warning at startup when the resolved address is not loopback, and it does not
# refuse, because putting a tunnel in front of it is how it is meant to be
# published.
#
# The host is required. ":8080" is refused rather than treated as every
# interface, because that spelling is the easiest way to expose this service
# without meaning to, and an empty value is treated as unset — so a broken
# template substitution gets the loopback default instead of a wider one.
#
# The MCP SDK's DNS-rebinding protection applies only when the listener itself is
# on a loopback address: then a request whose Host header is not loopback is
# refused with 403. That is still the rule for someone running the binary at this
# default. In the container deployment, however, this value is set to
# 0.0.0.0:8080, so the listener is not loopback and the check is inert; the
# cloudflared container can send its ordinary Host header over the shared network.
CERBERUS_MCP_ADDRESS=

# Where the MCP endpoint is mounted. Must begin with "/". Default /mcp.
# It is one whole path and not a prefix: the SDK's handler serves one endpoint.
CERBERUS_MCP_PATH=

# How long a shutdown waits for in-flight calls after SIGINT or SIGTERM before it
# stops waiting and closes the pools anyway. Default 30s.
#
# The default is longer than CERBERUS_DB_QUERY_TIMEOUT plus CERBERUS_DB_TIMEOUT_GRACE
# so that an ordinary in-flight query finishes on its own rather than being cut
# off by our own shutdown. If you raise either of those, raise this too. It stays
# a bound in any case: a client holding a connection open cannot delay a restart
# indefinitely.
CERBERUS_MCP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=

# The audit stream is always written to stdout as JSON and is tagged
# stream=audit. Its permissions, retention and rotation belong to the Docker
# configuration on the server rather than to this process.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication. Neither of these has a default and the process refuses to start
# without both, so this server cannot be deployed unauthenticated by forgetting
# something. Neither is a secret: what a caller presents is, and that value is
# never logged in any form.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Google OAuth client ID whose access tokens this deployment accepts. It
# looks like <digits>-<opaque>.apps.googleusercontent.com.
#
# Every request must carry a Google OAuth access token, and a token is only
# usable here when Google's own answer about it says it was issued to this exact
# client. That check is what stops a token minted for some other application —
# another connector in the same Google project, anything a colleague configured
# for themselves — from being replayed at this server to read the aliases above.
#
# Absent, the process refuses to start and names this variable. A value with a
# space or a newline in it is refused rather than trimmed: that value is a paste
# broken across two lines or a file read with its newline attached, and accepting
# it produces a server that answers 401 to a perfectly correct token for a reason
# no log line can name.
#
# It is a public identifier, and this process logs it unredacted at startup on
# purpose — a client ID that does not match the one the agent was configured with
# is otherwise a silent 401 on every single request. There is no client secret
# here and no variable for one, because checking somebody else's token does not
# need one. Nothing in this process issues, refreshes, proxies or stores a token.
CERBERUS_AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# The verified Google addresses that may reach a tool, comma-separated.
#
# This is the whole authorization model: a token Google vouches for gets a caller
# as far as this list, and the list decides. Entries are trimmed and matched
# case-insensitively against the address Google returns, a trailing comma is
# ignored, and `email_verified` must be true on Google's answer — an address that
# matches but is unverified is refused, which for an ordinary Google or Workspace
# account never happens and for one where it does happen means something about
# that account is unfinished.
#
# The two refusals are different statuses and it is worth knowing which you are
# looking at. A token Google will not vouch for is 401: the caller can change the
# answer by presenting a different one. A valid token whose identity is not on
# this list is 403, and nothing the caller does will change it — somebody has to
# add the address here. The refusal is logged with the exact address Google
# returned, so the value to paste in is in the log rather than guessed at.
#
# An empty or absent list refuses to start. Neither reading of an empty list is
# safe enough to pick: "everyone" is these databases on the public internet, and
# "nobody" is a server that starts, logs nothing unusual and answers 403 to its
# only user. For the same reason an entry that cannot ever match — a bare domain
# written by somebody expecting it to admit everyone in it, two addresses
# separated by a space instead of a comma, an @ left out — is refused at startup
# rather than parsed into a list that silently admits fewer people than it reads
# like it does.
#
# The list is read once, at startup, and echoed into the startup log exactly as it
# was written here — which is where a stray space, a capitalised domain that turned
# out not to matter, or an entry a template substitution ate can be read off
# directly. Adding or removing an address takes a restart, which also empties the
# validation cache described below.
CERBERUS_AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Google client behind those two variables. Nothing to set here; this is how
# a value for CERBERUS_AUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID comes to exist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. Create a DEDICATED OAuth client of type "Web application" in Google Cloud,
#    separate from any other client this project uses. Separate so that its
#    tokens are only good here: the audience check above is worth something
#    exactly to the degree that no other application shares the client.
# 2. Consent screen: External user type, publication status Testing, and add
#    every person who will connect as a test user. Scopes are identity only —
#    openid, email, profile. Nothing here reads a mailbox, a calendar or a
#    Drive, and no scope beyond those three is used for anything.
# 3. The redirect URI is not ours to choose. The MCP client — ChatGPT's or
#    Claude's connector setup screen — displays the callback URL it will use;
#    paste that exact string into the client's authorized redirect URIs. A
#    mismatch fails in Google's UI during the connection, before this server is
#    involved at all.
# 4. The MCP client holds both the client ID and the client secret and performs
#    the whole authorization-code flow. This server holds neither secret: only
#    the public client ID, in the variable above. There is no OAuth metadata
#    endpoint here, no dynamic client registration and no .well-known document.
# 5. Validation goes to https://oauth2.googleapis.com/tokeninfo over HTTPS, and
#    it is the only outbound host this process talks to besides your databases.
#    Egress to it has to be open wherever this runs, or every request is a 401
#    with a "validation_unavailable" class in the log.
#
# Three things about this arrangement will surprise an operator, and all three
# are deliberate:
#
# Testing publication status has a user cap and a short refresh-token lifetime.
# The connector will therefore ask to reauthorize periodically — that is not a
# fault, and it is worth triggering once on purpose so that it is a known event
# rather than an outage. The cap is also a second bound on who can ever get a
# token for this client at all.
#
# Do NOT publish the consent screen. Publishing means any Google account on
# earth can complete the flow and hold a valid token for this client, and then
# the only thing between it and these databases is CERBERUS_AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS.
# That allowlist exists precisely because Google's half of this boundary is a
# question about a client, not about a person; keeping the consent screen in
# Testing is what keeps the two halves independent.
#
# Revoking a grant is not instant here. A token this process has already accepted
# stays accepted for up to five minutes, the lifetime of an in-process cache that
# exists so the volume against Google's endpoint stays near one request per token
# per five minutes — Google documents that endpoint as subject to throttling. So
# revoking access in Google Account permissions leaves a tail. For immediate
# containment, remove the address from CERBERUS_AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS and restart,
# which drops the cache with the process; deleting or rotating the OAuth client
# in Google Cloud is the heavier hammer and invalidates every token at once.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variables that are not ours, and that this service refuses to start on.
# Nothing to set here. This section exists so that the refusal is findable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The three below belong to the database drivers, not to this service, and each is
# something a driver reads out of the environment behind our back. Configuration
# here comes from CERBERUS_DB_* and nothing else, so rather than let one of them
# quietly win, the process refuses to start and names the one it found.
#
# The refusal is deliberately narrow. It fires only when an alias actually uses the
# driver that reads the variable — a PostgreSQL alias for the first two, a SQL
# Server alias for the third — and only when the value is non-empty, because every
# one of these drivers ignores its own variable set to the empty string. So an
# operator who has none of the matching aliases configured is not affected at all.
#
# PGSERVICE      Names an entry in a pg_service.conf file. pgx decides to read
#                that file on the presence of the key alone, so no value in the
#                connection string we build can prevent it: with PGSERVICE set,
#                a file would answer configuration questions, and a missing or
#                unreadable file makes every PostgreSQL alias fail to open. The
#                likeliest way to hit this is having it exported for your own
#                psql — which is exactly why the message names the variable
#                instead of failing somewhere further in.
# PGSERVICEFILE  The path to that file. Inert on its own, since the file is only
#                read when a service is also named, but it is the other half of
#                one mechanism and the half whose value is a path.
# MSSQL_USE_EPA  Turns Extended Protection negotiation on or off during SQL
#                Server login. Not a file and not a credential, so it is a
#                weaker case than the two above — it is refused because what
#                this process negotiates with a third party's server should not
#                be decided by an unrelated variable in the shell that launched
#                it, and because a value the driver cannot parse fails the
#                connection with the value quoted back in the error.
#
# If you need one of these set for other tools in the same environment, set it
# where those tools run rather than where this service does. The refusal is a
# refusal rather than an override on purpose: unsetting a variable somebody
# deliberately exported would be a silent side effect on them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Integration tests only. Not read by the service.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The tests behind `-tags integration` take their connections from the same
# variables above, so they assume nothing about where any server lives. Aliases
# for PostgreSQL and MySQL can point at deploy/compose.test.yaml; a SQL Server
# alias points at whatever instance is reachable, and the SQL Server tests skip
# with a clear message when none is configured.
#
# The engines a run insists on, comma-separated, spelled as CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_ENGINE
# spells them. An engine named here turns every skip that would have covered it —
# no configuration, no alias, an alias that cannot be reached — into a failure.
#
# It exists because each of those skips is individually right, and together they
# mean a typo in one CERBERUS_DB_* variable name gives exit 0 with nothing
# asserted, which reads exactly like the legitimate SQL Server skip. Five of this
# layer's ten acceptance criteria turn on that distinction. CI sets it to
# postgresql,mysql — the two engines it has containers for; SQL Server is absent
# because no instance is reachable from a runner.
#
# Unset, every engine is optional, and that default is deliberate: it is what keeps
# the suite runnable by a developer with no containers at all. Set it locally to
# whatever you have running, and a typo becomes a failure instead of silence.
CERBERUS_TEST_REQUIRE_ENGINES=

# The statement used to provoke a timeout against SQL Server. It defaults to
# WAITFOR DELAY '00:00:30', which takes no locks, reads no page and burns no CPU.
# Override it for an instance where even that is unwelcome.
CERBERUS_TEST_MSSQL_SLOW_STATEMENT=

# The sqlserver alias the SQL Server catalog tests run against — the search_schema
# and describe_table statements — spelled as it is declared in CERBERUS_DB_ALIASES,
# hyphens and all, and not as it is spelled inside the CERBERUS_DB_<ALIAS>_*
# variable names. For an alias declared as my-alias holding one database in
# CERBERUS_DB_MY_ALIAS_DATABASES, my-alias is the value here; the tests find the
# my-alias.<database> connection from it.
#
# It is asked for by name rather than taken from whichever sqlserver alias comes
# first, because more than one is normally configured and an assertion about a
# catalog is an assertion about one server specifically.
#
# There is deliberately no default: the catalog those tests read belongs to a third
# party, and their alias is an identifier of theirs like any other: it belongs in an
# operator's own .env and in no committed file. Unset — or naming an alias that is
# not configured — the SQL Server catalog tests skip and say which of the two it is.
CERBERUS_TEST_SQLSERVER_ALIAS=
